I do understand this argument, and I do believe that allowing your pet
to take *some* of the fights and get beefed up in the early game is
helpful. I know that player character XL contributes to monster
strength, but so does DL. The question really isn't, "How tough are
the monsters that I'm going to encounter?" It's, "How tough are the
monsters in comparison with my character?"

Let's say you get down to DL 9 and at the same time level up to XL 9
(2560 XP). The hardest monsters you'll be fighting will be of
difficulty level (DIF) 9: winter wolf, hell hound pup, glass piercer,
large mimic, steam vortex, long worm, ogre lord. Your character will
have at least 85 hp and all the armor he has found exploring down to
DL 9.

Now let's say you get down to DL 9 at XL 1. The hardest monsters
you'll be fighting will be of DIF 5: gnome mummies, gnomish wizards,
Mordor orcs, Uruk-hai, bugbears, killer bees, and nymphs. Your
character is likely to have 10-15 max hp, and the same armor as he
would have had at XL 9, because he's explored the same territory.
Personally, I'd say the XL 1 character is far more overmatched than
the XL 9 character. Many of these monsters can take you out in two
hits, and some are pack hunters.

(You may say that you wouldn't push the 
avoiding-levelling-up-strategy that far, but this was just an
illustration for comparison. Anyway, if you're making for Minetown, it
can be generated as low as DL 8, and the Mines are harder than the
main dungeon anyway - which is another bad feature of the protection
racket: it draws you into the Mines when you're not ready to deal with
them.)
>
>Its easy to rocket up through XPlevels, you can be XP level 5 
>before even setting foot on Dlvl 4, and 9 comes quick if you hack 
>your way to gnome-town.  And thats where you will encounter a 
>problem. The longer you spend at a lower XP level, the more time 
>you'll have to find armor, weapons and other helpful items. 

The problem is that time is not what provides you with armor and
equipment. Exploration does. As I've shown above, you can have
explored the same amount of territory while levelling up as you can
while remaining at XL 1.

>XPlvl 
>9 doesn't do much good when you are in your starting armor, with 
>your starting weapon and a some serious monsters start to appear,  
>monsters your pet can't/won't help you with.  

Yes, but you're not going to have your starting armor/weapon by XL 9.
You're not going to kill 2560 XP worth of monsters without finding
some armor and weapons. Plus, I'm certainly not advocating the
opposite extreme: hanging around DL 1 and waiting for monsters to be
generated so I can level up before descending. That's almost as bad a
strategy as the protection racket.

>Leocrottas, 
>Owlbear, Soldier Ants all come to mind, maybe three-four fooElves 
>all with broadswords, bows and armor too, thats nasty.

Leocrottas are DIF 8; you're not going to meet them until the average
of XL and DL is 8. If you level up at roughly the same speed as you
descend down (which I find pretty usual in the early game), you should
have decent armor *and* decent hp before you meet them. Similar logic
applies to owlbears (DIF 7). Soldier ants (DIF 6) can be a problem,
but they respect Elbereth, and with 2d4 3d4P attacks per turn, they're
among the monsters that can kill you in 1-2 turns if you're a low
level character. Similar logic applies to elves; and as for their
having armor and weapons, well, that's where you tend to get good
equipment, from monsters that were first using it against you.

I recognize that these were merely examples. My point is that just as
it's foolhardy to level up a lot without getting proper armor, it's
foolhardy to plunge deep without getting proper hp. It's all about
balance.
>
>Sure I make exaggerations, but I see definate advantages to 
>prolonging the early XP levels, and it certainly doesn't have 
>everything to do with buying protection.

You make a good argument. I want to stress again: I'm not opposed to
people using this strategy if they find it works for them. I'm only
opposed to it being recommended to newbies as though it were the only
strategy worth considering.
